endobj
386 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 257 0 R
/T (Deaf and speech-impaired individuals face significant communication challenges in their daily lives, as they primarily rely on sign language, which is not widely understood by the majority of the population that uses spoken and written language for communication. This communication gap creates a strong need for technological tools capable of accurately interpreting sign language. Although considerable research has been conducted on global sign languages, there is still scope for improving intelligent and efficient recognition systems. This project focuses on developing a machine learning-based system for recognizing American Sign Language \(ASL\) gestures. A dataset was created using a combination of two-dimensional and three-dimensional images representing selected static ASL alphabet gestures. The MediaPipe framework was implemented to detect and extract hand landmarks from the gesture images, capturing key positional features of the fingers and palm. These extracted features were used to train a feed-forward neural network model for gesture classification. The trained model achieved high accuracy in recognizing the selected ASL gestures. The results demonstrate that the proposed approach is effective for sign language recognition and can be further extended to include dynamic gestures, full alphabet sets, and real-time sign-to-text or sign-to-speech conversion systems)
/E (Deaf and speech-impaired individuals face significant communication challenges in their daily lives, as they primarily rely on sign language, which is not widely understood by the majority of the population that uses spoken and written language for communication. This communication gap creates a strong need for technological tools capable of accurately interpreting sign language. Although considerable research has been conducted on global sign languages, there is still scope for improving intelligent and efficient recognition systems. This project focuses on developing a machine learning-based system for recognizing American Sign Language \(ASL\) gestures. A dataset was created using a combination of two-dimensional and three-dimensional images representing selected static ASL alphabet gestures. The MediaPipe framework was implemented to detect and extract hand landmarks from the gesture images, capturing key positional features of the fingers and palm. These extracted features were used to train a feed-forward neural network model for gesture classification. The trained model achieved high accuracy in recognizing the selected ASL gestures. The results demonstrate that the proposed approach is effective for sign language recognition and can be further extended to include dynamic gestures, full alphabet sets, and real-time sign-to-text or sign-to-speech conversion systems)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
